    "prompt_mask": "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{@@n@@}{3x}?$",
    "ordered_solution_mask": "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{@@n@@}{3}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{@@n@@}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{@@n@@}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{@@n@@}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=\\frac{@@n@@}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $\\frac{@@n@@}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{@@n@@}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{@@n@@}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{@@n@@}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+\\frac{@@n@@}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061K \\\\  & =\\frac{@@n@@}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061K) \\\\  & =\\frac{@@n@@}{3}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
