    "prompt_mask": "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{@@n@@}{2x})}^{2}?$",
    "ordered_solution_mask": "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{@@n@@}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{@@n@@}{x}+\\frac{@@square@@}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+@@n@@\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{@@square@@}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+@@n@@ln\u2061|x|+\\frac{@@square@@{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+@@n@@ln\u2061|x|-\\frac{@@square@@}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=@@n@@ln\u2061K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $@@n@@ln\u2061|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{@@n@@}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{@@square@@}{4x}+@@n@@ln\u2061|x|+@@n@@ln\u2061K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{@@square@@}{4x}+@@n@@(ln\u2061|x|+ln\u2061K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{@@square@@}{4x}+@@n@@ln\u2061(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
