    "prompt_mask": "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{@@n@@x-1}{x}?$",
    "ordered_solution_mask": "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{@@n@@x-1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{@@n@@x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (@@n@@-\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =@@n@@\\int \\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =@@n@@x-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{@@n@@x-1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =@@n@@x-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C \\\\  & =@@n@@x-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061K \\\\  & =@@n@@x-(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061K) \\\\  & =@@n@@x-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
