    "prompt_mask": "What is the antiderivative of $@@n@@{x}^{-1}?$",
    "ordered_solution_mask": "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $@@n@@$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int @@n@@{x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =@@n@@\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =@@n@@ln\u2061|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=@@n@@ln\u2061K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $@@n@@ln\u2061|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int @@n@@{x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =@@n@@ln\u2061|x|+2ln\u2061K \\\\  & =@@n@@(ln\u2061|x|+ln\u2061K) \\\\  & =@@n@@ln\u2061(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
