    "ordered_solution_mask": "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{@@n@@}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{@@twice@@}{x}+\\frac{@@square@@}{{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+@@twice@@\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+@@squarecoef@@\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+@@twice@@ln\u2061|x|+\\frac{@@squarecoef@@{x}^{-2+1}}{-2+1}+C \\\\  & =x+@@twice@@ln\u2061|x|-\\frac{@@square@@}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=@@twice@@ln\u2061K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $@@twice@@ln\u2061|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{@@n@@}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{@@square@@}{x}+@@twice@@ln\u2061|x|+@@twice@@ln\u2061K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{@@square@@}{x}+@@twice@@(ln\u2061|x|+ln\u2061K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{@@square@@}{x}+@@twice@@ln\u2061(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
